\description{
This function \strong{links the two grafting processes} of phylograftR in one run, with a unique set of inputs:
the missing taxa are grafted into their target clades with \code{\link[=graft_missing_taxa]{graft_missing_taxa()}},
and the missing clades are grafted as sisters of backbone clades with \code{\link[=graft_missing_clades]{graft_missing_clades()}}.
By default the missing taxa are grafted first and the missing clades second, but the order can be chosen with \code{grafting_order}.

For each grafted phylogeny of the first step, \strong{the second step is run on this phylogeny used as backbone}.
With several stochastic maps and/or several simulations, the output is thus the same set of \verb{P maps x N simulations} grafted phylogenies as with one of the two functions,
but \strong{each phylogeny includes both the grafted taxa and the grafted clades}.

The grafted tips and branches of the output (\code{grafted_tips} and \code{grafted_edges}) identify all the grafted taxa, from the two steps.
}
\details{
\subsection{Why use this function}{

Grafting the taxa and then the clades by hand is simple for \strong{one} grafted phylogeny: the output of the first function is the \code{phy} of the second.
It becomes laborious with several simulations and/or stochastic maps, because each phylogeny must be chained with its own simmap and seed.
This function does it in one call, with a single seed, and the output has the same structure as the output of the individual functions.
}

\subsection{Order of the steps}{

With the default \code{grafting_order = c("taxa", "clades")}, the taxa are grafted into their target clades first.
\strong{The grafted taxa are then treated as backbone species by the second step} (e.g., they count as tips of the sister clades, and can be part of the clade used to fit the birth-death model).
With \code{grafting_order = c("clades", "taxa")} the missing clades are grafted first, and the taxa can then be grafted within them.
The two orders do not give the same phylogenies, but give the same sampling fractions if \code{total_richness_per_clades} is the true richness of the clades.
}

\subsection{Inputs}{

The inputs are the same as the ones of the individual functions, with the lists of the two types of taxa provided together
(\code{missing_taxa_list}, \code{total_richness_per_clades}). \strong{The function stops, before any grafting,} if these lists are not valid or if taxon names are duplicated,
if \code{phy} is not ultrametric or binary, or if \code{simmap} is not valid. As for the individual functions, the other constraints are checked for each step (the inputs of the second step
with the phylogeny of the first step), so some errors can be raised during the first simulation.
}

\subsection{Multiple simulations}{

A \strong{simulation} is the grafting of both types of taxa, one after the other. All the simulations are produced as in the individual functions:
one phylogeny with \code{nb_simulations = 1}, a \code{"multiPhylo"} named \code{"sim_1"}, \code{"sim_2"}, ... with several, with the attributes \code{"seeds"} (seed of each phylogeny) and \code{"failed"}.
\strong{Both steps of a simulation use the same seed}, which is also the \code{"seed"} attribute of the phylogeny, so a phylogeny can be reproduced alone with its seed.

A failure that is specific to a simulation (e.g., no usable divergence age after \code{max_age_redraws} redraws) at \strong{either step} skips the whole simulation with a warning.
The data.frame in the attribute \code{"failed"} has the columns \code{seed}, \code{reason} and \strong{\code{step}} (\code{"taxa"} or \code{"clades"}). With \code{force_simulations = TRUE},
new seeds are tried until \code{nb_simulations} complete phylogenies are obtained (up to \code{10 * nb_simulations} attempts), redoing both steps for each attempt.
Any other error (invalid inputs, no branch available for a taxon, ...) stops the whole run.
}

\subsection{Multiple stochastic maps}{

With a \code{"multiSimmap"}, every map is used for \code{nb_simulations} simulations, and the output is a list with one element per map, as in \code{\link[=graft_missing_taxa]{graft_missing_taxa()}}
(with the attribute \code{"failed_maps"}). \strong{Each simulation uses the same map for both steps}: the updated map of the first step constrains the second step.
Maps of several traits are paired as in the individual functions.
}

\subsection{Temporary saves and resuming}{

With \code{save_temp = TRUE}, each step of each phylogeny has its own temporary file (suffix \code{"_step_taxa"} or \code{"_step_clades"}, in addition to the suffixes of the maps and simulations).
A run interrupted during the second step of a simulation resumes from the temporary file of this step, and the first step is reused from its complete file.
The files are deleted at the end of the run if \code{clean_temp_files = TRUE}, and \strong{never} if the run crashes. The arguments must be exactly the same to resume.
}

\subsection{Parallel computation}{

The simulations (all maps included) are run as independent tasks, each task doing both steps. The results do not depend on whether they are run in parallel or not.
See \code{\link[=graft_missing_taxa]{graft_missing_taxa()}} for recommendations.
}
}
\examples{
# --- Prepare data --- #

# Create a toy backbone phylogeny: ultrametric, with a root age of 50 time units
set.seed(1234)
phy <- ape::rcoal(n = 30)
phy$edge.length <- phy$edge.length / max(ape::node.depth.edgelength(phy)) * 50

# The missing taxa and the missing clades are provided together
missing_taxa_list <- list(
  # Taxa grafted in their target clades
  taxa = list(Taxon_name = c("X1", "X2", "X3"),
              Target_clade = list(X1 = c("t26", "t18"), X2 = c("t8", "t22"), X3 = "t5")),
  # Taxa grafted as sister clades. M1 and M2 share the same sister clade: they are grafted together
  clades = list(Taxon_name = c("M1", "M2", "M3"),
                Sister_clade = list(M1 = c("t8", "t22"), M2 = c("t8", "t22"), M3 = c("t26", "t18"))))

# --- Perform grafting of both types of missing lineages at once --- #

# Graft the taxa, then the missing clades, in one call
grafted_phy <- graft_missing_taxa_and_clades(phy = phy, missing_taxa_list = missing_taxa_list,
                                             verbose = FALSE, seed = 1234)
# All the grafted taxa are identified
sum(grafted_phy$grafted_tips)
plot_grafted(grafted_phy, cex = 0.7)

# Graft the missing clades first, then the taxa
grafted_phy_clades_first <- graft_missing_taxa_and_clades(
  phy = phy, missing_taxa_list = missing_taxa_list, grafting_order = c("clades", "taxa"),
  verbose = FALSE, seed = 1234)

# --- Produce several grafted phylogenies --- #

# Each of the 3 simulations grafts both types of missing taxa, with a single seed for both steps
grafted_phys <- graft_missing_taxa_and_clades(phy = phy, missing_taxa_list = missing_taxa_list,
                                              nb_simulations = 3, verbose = FALSE, seed = 1234)
names(grafted_phys)
attr(grafted_phys, "seeds")
sapply(grafted_phys, function (x) sum(x$grafted_tips))

# --- Account for unsampled species --- #

# Provide the total richness of the clades of both types of taxa
# to account for unsampled species (not in the backbone, nor grafted)
total_richness_per_clades <- list(
  taxa = list(Target_clade = list(c("t26", "t18"), c("t8", "t22"), "t5"), total_richness = c(30, 25, 4)),
  clades = list(Sister_clade = list(c("t8", "t22"), c("t26", "t18")), total_richness = c(25, NA)))

# Graft while accounting for unsampled species
# (Modify the sampling fractions used during fitting)
grafted_phy_richness <- graft_missing_taxa_and_clades(
  phy = phy, missing_taxa_list = missing_taxa_list,
  total_richness_per_clades = total_richness_per_clades, return_BD_model_summary = TRUE,
  verbose = FALSE, seed = 1234)
# Inspect info for birth-death models of both steps
extract_BD_model_summary(grafted_phy_richness)[, c("step", "b", "d", "rho")]

# --- Use several stochastic maps as constraints --- #

# Load backbone and simmap
data(toy_backbone)
data(toy_simmap)

# Extract tip states
tip_states <- phytools::getStates(toy_simmap, type = "tips")

# Produce multiple simmaps conditioned to the tip states
set.seed(118)
toy_multiSimmap <- phytools::make.simmap(toy_backbone, tip_states,
                                         model = "ER", nsim = 2, message = FALSE)

# Build shared missing_taxa_list
toy_missing_list <- list(
  taxa = list(Taxon_name = c("X1", "X2"), Target_clade = list(X1 = c("t26", "t18"), X2 = c("t26", "t18"))),
  clades = list(Taxon_name = c("M1", "M2"), Sister_clade = list(M1 = "t14", M2 = "t14")))

# Produce grafted phylogenies using the same maps to constrain both steps
# Here, we only allow grafting on "green" portions of branches
grafted_phys_maps <- graft_missing_taxa_and_clades(
  phy = toy_backbone, missing_taxa_list = toy_missing_list, simmap = toy_multiSimmap,
  allowed_states = "green", nb_simulations = 2, verbose = FALSE, seed = 1234)

# Output: a list with one element per map, each a multiPhylo with the simulations of the map
names(grafted_phys_maps)
names(grafted_phys_maps$map_2)


}
